pkg/proc, pkg/terminal: shared addresses, return names, goroutine frames

An address stops for one breakpoint, and two functions share an address
when one is inlined into the other at its first statement, as
(*T).Free calling release. After "trace a pkg.(*T).Free", "trace b
pkg.(*T).release" printed a bare "Breakpoint exists" when the last of
b's addresses was a's, even though b's other addresses had been set,
and those were left behind without their logical breakpoint; when
another address was last, b was set silently without a's. Now a
breakpoint is set when it gets any of its addresses, the terminal says
which addresses another breakpoint has:

	tracepoint b does not stop at 0x49a2a8, which tracepoint a has: an address stops for one breakpoint, and the stack at its stops shows every call inlined there

and when every address is taken the error names the breakpoint that
has it ("Breakpoint exists at FILE:LINE at ADDR: tracepoint a on
pkg.(*T).Free has it, ..."). A breakpoint on a function's returns that
another tracepoint already has no longer fails the trace command.

At such an address, a hit was labeled with the innermost inlined call,
so tracepoint a printed release. The debugger now picks, among the calls
inlined at the PC and the function they were inlined into, the call of
the function the breakpoint was set on: api.Thread.Inlined, File and
Line, and the arguments the breakpoint loads, are that call's.

A tracepoint's return lines and records carried no name of their own:
in the JSON output a return record's "bp" was the ID of the breakpoint
on the return, so a filter on the tracepoint's name missed the returns.
They now carry the tracepoint's name (or its ID), in text as on its
entries:

	>> goroutine(16): [w] main.work => (...)

In the JSON output, goroutines -t N left out the stacks and -exec CMD
did not run CMD; each goroutine now has "frames", as the stack record,
and CMD's records follow.

All are generic fixes for upstream Delve, the JSON ones for the output
mode of 0003.

